  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY NIGHT THROUGH THURSDAY/... \nIssued at 1203 PM MDT Thu Aug 13 2026\n\nActive monsoon plume remains over the area through the weekend\nwith daily thunderstorm chances over the mountains and plains\neach day. Probabilities for flash flooding will continue and\nperhaps amplify as more areas see rainfall and antecedent soil\nmoistures increase. Precipitable waters remain in the 100-130%\nof normal range, highest across northern and eastern portions \nof the area and Saturday looks like an uptick in thunderstorms \nfor the southeast mountains and adjacent plains with the \npossibility for more Flash Flood watches to follow. CAPE \nincreases over the weekend as well with some marginal shear \n25-30 kts by Sunday suggesting some strong to marginally severe \nthunderstorms will be possible over portions of the southeast \nplains. Otherwise, temperatures will remain below normal with \nhighs in the 80s. \n\nPrecipitable waters decrease a bit, but still remain around\n100-120% of normal early next week. Upper high center shifts\nwestward, putting CO under a more westerly flow pattern aloft\nand shunting the tap of more significant moisture off to the\nsouth and west. But there is still sufficient moisture trapped\nwithin the upper high to bring daily thunderstorm chances,\nespecially to the mountains. Heavy rainfall may still accompany\nthe stronger storms. Temperatures heat up again towards mid \nweek.\n\nThere are some hints of a pattern change through late week as we\ncome under the influence of more westerly flow aloft with a\npassing system to our north on Thursday. Operational GFS appears\nto be an outlier compared to it ensemble mean and the EC camp. \nBut even the EC brings a front into the southeast plains \nsuggesting a return of low level moisture and another uptick in\nthunderstorms, albeit with more ridging aloft than GFS shows.\nEither way will stick close to the NBM model blends which keep\nhighs in the 90s across the plains and isolated to scattered\npops district-wide. -KT",
